  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos\u2061x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13933487/assets/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nNotice that our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. If we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture. This means that $cos\u2061x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos\u2061(-x)=cos\u2061x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061(-x) & =cos\u2061x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
